##:
net/dns -- a host name, resolved into typed addresses.

The module ships as bundled .sushi source and is merged as a compilation unit
when imported (`use <net/dns>`). It is sock_dns_resolve() from <net/socket>
with parse_ip() from <net/ip> over each answer, so a caller is handed IpAddr
and never a string it has to read for itself.

An answer the parser cannot read is dropped rather than failing the call, so
one unusual family does not lose the usable answers.

Limitations: the order of the answers is the resolver's and is not stable
between calls. A numeric text answers itself and makes no network request.
There is no reverse lookup and no SRV.
:##

use <net/socket>
use <net/ip>
public use <net/error>

##:
Resolves a host name, or a numeric address, into typed addresses.

A text that is already an address answers with exactly that address and makes
no network request.

- Parameter host: The name or numeric address to resolve.
- Returns: One or more addresses, in the resolver's order.
- Errors: NetError, when the name does not resolve.
:##
public fn resolve(string host) IpAddr[] | NetError:
    let string[] answers = sock_dns_resolve(host)??
    let IpAddr[] addresses = from([])
    foreach(text in answers.iter()):
        match parse_ip(text):
            Result.Ok(a) ->
                addresses.push(a)
            Result.Err(_) ->
                ~
    return Result.Ok(addresses)

##:
The first address of one family that the resolver gave.

- Parameter host: The name or numeric address to resolve.
- Parameter want_v4: True for the first IPv4 answer, false for the first IPv6 one.
- Returns: The first address of that family.
- Errors: NetError.InvalidAddress, when no answer is of that family, or the
  resolver's own error.
:##
public fn resolve_first(string host, bool want_v4) IpAddr | NetError:
    let IpAddr[] addresses = resolve(host)??
    foreach(a in addresses.iter()):
        if (a.is_v4() == want_v4):
            return Result.Ok(a)
    return Result.Err(NetError.InvalidAddress)
